 ## Subtopics
 
-### Probabilistic Point Representation
-Models the point-wise feature $f_\mathbf{x}$ as a stochastic variable using an encoder-decoder structure and ELBO loss, allowing for robust handling of noise and variations [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Parameter Candidate Decoders (PCD)
-Parallel decoders that generate base parameters and masks for each target layer, fused with layer-variant codes to create a diverse pool of candidate weights [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Ray Transformer Renderer
-The underlying renderer architecture that uses self-attention along rays, with specific layers (Q, K, V, FFN) personalized per point [S1, unofficial].
+### Generalizable NeRFs
+Concatenate geometry-agnostic $[\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}]$ with geometry-aware $f_\mathbf{x}$ but keep parameters $\Theta$ fixed. OPONeRF conditions parameters on $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{F}$. [S1, unofficial]
+
+### StochasticNeRF
+Models static appearance as stochastic variables. OPONeRF targets the sampled point itself, subdividing the scene and personalizing point-wise parameters. [S1, unofficial]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Higher than standard NeRF due to the per-point parameter generation and probabilistic modeling. The cost volume construction and 3D CNN inference add overhead.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Increased memory usage for storing the overall scene representation $\mathbf{F}$, parameter candidates $\mathbf{W}^l_a$, and layer-variant codes $\mathbf{Z}^l$.
-*   **Performance:** Provides robustness to test-time scene perturbations (motion, illumination, contamination) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High expressiveness and flexibility due to point-wise personalization. Handles dynamic scenes better than static NeRFs.
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive. Requires careful tuning of the probabilistic modeling and diversity loss.
-*   **When to Use:** When robustness to test-time changes is critical, and computational resources are sufficient.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For static scenes where standard NeRF is sufficient, or when real-time performance is required.
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** The computational cost of the Parameter Candidate Decoders (PCD) is high for full-sized outputs, especially when layer widths are large ($C_i^l, C_o^l > 1000$). The mitigation strategy (down-sized output + linear upscaling + element-wise multiplication) reduces this cost but adds complexity.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the global scene representation $\mathbf{F}$, layer-variant codes $\mathbf{Z}^l$, and candidate parameters $\mathbf{W}^l_a$ for all target layers.
+*   **Strengths:** Handles test-time scene changes (motion, illumination, contamination) robustly by personalizing parameters per point. More expressive and flexible than fixed-parameter NeRFs.
+*   **Limitations:** High computational overhead due to the need to generate and select parameters for each point. The probabilistic modeling and diversity loss add to the training complexity.
+*   **When to use:** When robustness to test-time perturbations is critical and computational resources allow for the overhead of per-point parameter generation.
+*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is required and scene changes are minimal or non-existent.
 
 ## Background
-OPONeRF was introduced by Yu Zheng, Yueqi Duan, Kangfu Zheng, Hongru Yan, Jiwen Lu, and Jie Zhou. It builds on the concept of generalizable NeRFs but extends it by conditioning the network parameters on the sampled point itself, rather than just concatenating features with fixed parameters. The method addresses the limitations of StochasticNeRF, which models static appearance as stochastic variables, by targeting the sampled point directly [S1, unofficial].
+
+OPONeRF (One-Point-One NeRF) was introduced by Yu Zheng, Yueqi Duan, Kangfu Zheng, Hongru Yan, Jiwen Lu, and Jie Zhou. The method addresses the limitations of standard NeRFs and generalizable NeRFs in handling dynamic or perturbed scenes. It builds on the Ray Transformer architecture and incorporates ideas from probabilistic modeling and parameter-efficient fine-tuning. The method was presented in the paper "OPONeRF: One-Point-One NeRF for Robust Neural Rendering" [S1, unofficial].
 
